\f0\fs26 \cf0 Are we running out of our favorite Japanese food? Sushi is threatened by its own popularity. Once a niche, regionally specific cuisine, modern sushi has become a global product that has pushed its own ingredients to the brink. Sushi\'92s main player is the bluefin tuna, a fish the sushi industry has transformed from low-grade, low-cost product into one of the most valuable and overexploited meats on the planet. \
\
The Last Sushi in the World is an interactive website that asks a simple question: are we running out of sushi, and if so, when? My thesis work visualizes publicly available data from world fisheries, logistical chains, and technological advancements to answer this question. I use recognizable chart forms and maps to reveal data sequentially to readers. The important context for this data (the data around the data, if you will) is never far away from the visuals, guiding the reader through a deceptively complex and surprisingly hopeful narrative. Ultimately, this project aims to update the reader\'92s understanding of sushi, and familiarize themselves with the costs and futures of a cuisine enjoyed by many.\
\
The website can be viewed at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://nmolnar-parsons.github.io/thesis/"}}{\fldrslt nmolnar-parsons.github.io/thesis/}} (optimized for desktop). My full portfolio can be viewed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://nichosmolnar.github.io/portfolio/"}}{\fldrslt nichosmolnar.github.io/portfolio/}}. If you\'92re interested in the engineering or full written thesis of this project, this can be viewed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://github.com/nmolnar-parsons/thesis"}}{\fldrslt github.com/nmolnar-parsons/thesis}}. }